      "text": "Given that \\( z \\) is 60, we know that \\( y = 5z \\). To find the value of \\( y \\), we substitute \\( z = 60 \\) into the equation \\( y = 5z \\):\n\n\\[\ny = 5 \\cdot 60 = 300\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\( y \\), we need to find the value of \\( x \\). The value of \\( x \\) is one-half of the value of \\( y \\), so we can calculate \\( x \\) as follows:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1}{2} y\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( y = 300 \\) into the equation for \\( x \\), we get:\n\n\\[\nx = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 300 = 150\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( x \\) is \\(\\boxed{150}\\).\n\nLarry, Ricky, and Devin are all training for a marathon organized by a local NGO to support children in their town who have cancer. Larry runs 3 miles on days when it rains, while Devin runs 12 miles on days when it snows. If it rained 2 times as much as it snowed plus an extra 5 times in a month, and it snowed 2 miles every time it snowed, what is the total number of miles the three kids run in a month?\n\nTo find the total number of miles the three kids run in a month, we need to break down the information given and calculate the miles run by each kid on a day it snows and a day it rains.\n\nFirst, let's determine the number of times it snowed (S) and the number of times it rained (R) in a month. It is given that it rained 2 times as much as it snowed plus an extra 5 times, and it snowed 2 miles every time it snowed. Since it snowed 2 miles each time, the total number of times it snowed is \\( S = 2 \\) and the total number of times it rained is \\( R = 2S + 5 = 2 \\times 2 + 5 = 9 \\).\n\nNext, we calculate the total miles run by Devin and Larry:\n- Devin runs 12 miles on days it snows. Since it snowed \\((S+9)\\) times (total number of times it snows plus the extra 9 times) in the month, Devin runs \\(12(S+9)\\) miles in total.\n- Larry runs 3 miles on days it rains. Since it rained 9 times in the month, Larry runs \\(3 \\times 9 = 27\\) miles in total.\n\nNow, we calculate the total miles run by the three kids in total:\n- Company A runs \\(6(S+9)\\) miles. Substituting \\( S = 2 \\) into the expression, we get \\(6(2+9) = 6 \\times 11 = 66\\) miles.\n- Devin runs \\(12S + 12 \\times 2 = 12 \\times 2 + 12 \\times 2 = 24 + 24 = 48\\) miles.\n- Larry runs 27 miles.\n\nThe total miles run by the three kids is:\n\\[ 66 + 48 + 27 = 141 \\]\n\nSo, the total number of miles the three kids run in a month is \\(\\boxed{141}\\).\n\nWhen $a=\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3}+2\\right)(\\dfrac{5}{3}-2)$, find the value of the algebraic expression $a^{2}-4a+4$.\n\nTo solve the problem, we first need to determine the value of \\(a\\). Given the value of \\(a = \\left(\\dfrac{5}{3} + 2\\right)\\left(\\dfrac{5}{3} - 2\\right)\\), we start by simplifying the expression inside the parentheses.\n\nFirst, calculate \\(\\dfrac{5}{3} + 2\\):\n\\[\n\\dfrac{5}{3} + 2 = \\dfrac{5}{3} + \\dfrac{6}{3} = \\dfrac{11}{3}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate \\(\\dfrac{5}{3} - 2\\):\n\\[\n\\dfrac{5}{3} - 2 = \\dfrac{5}{3} - \\dfrac{6}{3} = -\\dfrac{1}{3}\n\\]\n\nNow, multiply these two results together:\n\\[\na = \\left(\\dfrac{11}{3}\\right)\\left(-\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right) = \\dfrac{11}{3} \\cdot -\\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{-11}{9}\n\\]\n\nWe are asked to find the value of the algebraic expression \\(a^2 - 4a + 4\\) with \\(a = -\\dfrac{11}{9}\\). First, we substitute \\(a\\) into the expression:\n\\[\na^2 - 4a + 4\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\) for \\(a\\):\n\\[\n\\left(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\right)^2 - 4\\left(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\right) + 4\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\left(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\right)^2\\):\n\\[\n\\left(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\right)^2 = \\dfrac{121}{81}\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\(4 \\left(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\right)\\):\n\\[\n4 \\left(-\\dfrac{11}{9}\\right) = -\\dfrac{44}{9}\n\\]\n\nTo add these terms, we need a common denominator. The common denominator for 81 and 9 is 81. Convert \\(-\\dfrac{44}{9}\\) to a fraction with denominator 81:\n\\[\n-\\dfrac{44}{9} = -\\dfrac{44 \\cdot 9}{9 \\cdot 9} = -\\dfrac{396}{81}\n\\]\n\nNow, add \\(\\dfrac{121}{81}\\) and \\(-\\dfrac{396}{81}\\):\n\\[\n\\dfrac{121}{81} - \\dfrac{396}{81} = \\dfrac{121 - 396}{81} = \\dfrac{-275}{81}\n\\]\n\nFinally, add 4. Convert 4 to a fraction with denominator 81:\n\\[\n4 = \\dfrac{4 \\cdot 81}{81} = \\dfrac{324}{81}\n\\]\n\nNow, add \\(\\dfrac{-275}{81}\\) and \\(\\dfrac{324}{81}\\):\n\\[\n\\dfrac{-275}{81} + \\dfrac{324}{81} = \\dfrac{-275 + 324}{81} = \\dfrac{49}{81}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of the expression \\(a^2 - 4a + 4\\) when \\(a = -\\dfrac{11}{9}\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\dfrac{49}{81}}\n\\]",
